WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.727 --> 00:00:11.755
Each pin marks where an Indigenous scholar assigned this term locates their own Nation. The map turns the course's scholars into places, and your notes into a record you keep.

2
00:00:11.755 --> 00:00:20.967
Read the caveat first. This is a self-contained teaching map with approximate reading anchors, not a legal or definitive boundary map.

3
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:28.937
Tap a pin. Meet the scholar, the Nation and place they name, the concept they carry, and the readings they anchor.

4
00:00:28.937 --> 00:00:38.785
Switch layers. Toggle between the administrative view and the Indigenous scholar anchors to feel how differently the same land can be described.

5
00:00:38.785 --> 00:00:48.243
Write what each place makes visible. The note box asks for your own words, one anchor at a time. A named place beats a general impression.

6
00:00:48.243 --> 00:00:54.977
Save your map notes. The save button makes a Word file of your selected anchor and your notes.

7
00:00:54.977 --> 00:01:04.374
Bring the map to Weeks 13 and 14. The final weeks return to your cartography. The more you note now, the more you have to work with then.

8
00:01:04.374 --> 00:01:11.796
This is private work. It is never seen or scored; the graded cartography work is submitted on Blackboard.

9
00:01:11.796 --> 00:01:17.908
Where next: Add the current week's scholar to your notes while the reading is fresh.
